## Summary

Issue #192: the OAuth approval page (`web/mcp/authorize.html`) told every client it "wants to read" and "can change nothing". That is untrue of the iPhone app, which makes edits.

- **What changed:** the request description that `internal/mcp/oauth.go` returns to the page now carries a flag, `changes`. It is true when the redirect is one of the app redirects listed in `appRedirects` (today only `com.heliosian.Heliosian:/oauth`). When the flag is set, `authorize.js` replaces "read" with "use" and "can change nothing" with "can change what you can change". Other clients keep the read-only wording.
- **Why this way:** a single yes/no flag decided from `appRedirects` was the simplest way to word the page per client, as the TODO item asked. No per-client wording table was added.
- **Rename on the page:** at ian's request the page now says "Heliosian" instead of "Helios" in the title, the heading and both sentences.
- **Test:** the existing iPhone-app sign-in test in `internal/mcp/mcp_test.go` now also checks the flag.
- **Left out:** the new wording was never checked in a browser. The MCP resource name "Helios School" was left alone, since it names the school.

